d":"fc530f0b-c619-4bc0-ad84-fc3b556cd833","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/299/docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.2.yaml","content":"id: test-rel01.2\ntitle: \"Test Suite: rel01.2 — sponge specification and differential test\"\nrelease: rel01.2\n\ntraces:\n  - rel01.2-uc001-sponge\n  - prd007-sponge R1\n  - prd007-sponge R2\n  - prd007-sponge R3\n  - prd007-sponge R4\n  - prd007-sponge R5\n  - prd007-sponge R6\n\npreconditions:\n  - ./bin/sponge exists (built via mage build).\n  - Reference binary sponge is installed via Homebrew (moreutils) and on PATH.\n  - Tests are run via go test ./cmd/sponge/... which invokes RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils.\n  - All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n  - \"sponge: each test creates an isolated temp directory. Comparison is on output file content (prd001-testutils R5) for tests with an output filename. Passthrough mode tests compare stdout byte-for-byte.\"\n\ntest_cases:\n  - name: sponge_small_stdin_to_file\n    description: 100 lines piped to sponge with an output filename; compare output file content.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"outfile.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"seq 1 100 output (lines 1 through 100, each followed by newline)\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file outfile.txt contains \"100 lines, 1\\n through 100\\n\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R1.1\n      - prd007-sponge R1.2\n      - prd007-sponge R2.1\n\n  - name: sponge_soak_before_write\n    description: 'Same input and output file (cat file | sponge file). Output must equal input, proving sponge did not truncate before reading. Setup: data.txt contains \"line1\\nline2\\nline3\\n\", Pipeline: cat data.txt | sponge data.txt'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"data.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"content of data.txt (fed via 'cat data.txt')\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file data.txt contains \"line1\\nline2\\nline3\\n\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R1.1\n      - prd007-sponge R2.5\n\n  - name: sponge_passthrough_stdout\n    description: No output filename; stdin content appears on stdout.\n    inputs:\n      args: []\n      stdin: \"hello world\\n\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: \"hello world\\n\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R4.1\n      - prd007-sponge R4.3\n\n  - name: sponge_append_mode\n    description: '-a flag prepends original file content before stdin content. Setup: existing.txt contains \"original line\\n\"'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-a\", \"existing.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"appended line\\n\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file existing.txt contains \"original line\\nappended line\\n\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R3.1\n      - prd007-sponge R3.3\n\n  - name: sponge_append_mode_no_existing_file\n    description: '-a with a non-existent output file behaves as default mode (creates new file). Setup: newfile.txt does not exist'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-a\", \"newfile.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"new content\\n\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file newfile.txt contains \"new content\\n\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R3.2\n\n  - name: sponge_large_stdin\n    description: Input >= 1 MB forces temp-file spill; output file content must match.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"large_out.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"generated payload of 1,048,576+ bytes (e.g., seq 1 50000)\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file large_out.txt contains \"identical to stdin content\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R1.3\n      - prd007-sponge R1.4\n\n  - name: sponge_output_file_exists_mode\n    description: 'Existing file has mode 0640; after sponge, file mode is preserved. Setup: protected.txt exists with mode 0640 and contains \"old content\\n\"'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"protected.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"new content\\n\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file protected.txt contains \"new content\\n\" with mode 0640'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R2.3\n\n  - name: sponge_empty_stdin\n    description: Zero bytes of stdin; output file created with zero bytes.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"empty_out.txt\"]\n      stdin: \"\"\n      env: []\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'expected file empty_out.txt contains \"\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd007-sponge R1.1\n      - prd007-sponge R2.1\n","numLines":140,"startLine":1,"totalLines":140}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VExWZBX1EUDRi1T6M3myVq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n**cmd/sponge/main.go** - Implements the sponge command per prd007-sponge:\n- R1: Read all of stdin into a buffer before opening the output file\n- R1.2: Buffer stdin in memory using a dynamically grown buffer\n- R1.3: Spill to temp file when buffer gets too large\n- R1.4: Temp file in TMPDIR or /tmp with \"sponge.XXXXXX\" pattern\n- R1.5: Cleanup handler for temp file on signal\n\n\n- R2: Output file handling with atomic rename, fallback to copy, and permission preservation\n- R3: Append mode (-a)\n- R4: Passthrough mode (no output filename) - write to stdout\n- R5: Exit codes and error handling\n\n**cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go** - Differential tests using the reference binary when available, with fallback to direct functional tests covering stdin-to-stdout, file overwrite, empty input, binary data, and append mode scenarios.\n\nNow I'll implement these files.\n\nLooking at the requirements more carefully, I need to handle the buffering strategy with an initial 8KB buffer that doubles on overflow, spilling to a temp file in TMPDIR or /tmp when memory limits are exceeded. For output, I'll use atomic rename with a byte-for-byte copy fallback, preserving file modes via lstat. The append mode needs to prepend the original file content before stdin, treating non-existent files as a no-op. I should also ensure the temp file is cleaned up properly and never leave partially-written output.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the sponge command itself. My approach will be to parse the -a flag first, then read all stdin into the buffer with spillover handling, check file permissions if an output filename is provided, handle the append case by copying the original content to the temp file first, and finally write the output atomically.\n\nSetting up the Go package structure...\n\nNow I'm extracting the command-line arguments to get the output filename, then reading all of stdin into a buffer or temp file depending on size. I'll set up signal handlers to clean up any temporary files, then either write to stdout in passthrough mode or write to the specified file with append support.\n\nI need to reconsider the buffer strategy though—the PRD specifies starting with 8192 bytes and doubling when it overflows, spilling to a temp file once a memory threshold is hit. And looking at the test requirements, I should cover stdin-to-stdout passthrough, file overwriting, empty input, and binary data handling using the test utilities.\n\nThe tricky part is that DiffTest compares stdout and stderr, but sponge's main behavior is writing to files. So for file-mode tests I'll need custom logic to verify the file contents after running the binary, while using DiffTest for the passthrough cases where output goes to stdout. DiffTest works well for passthrough mode where I can compare stdout directly, but for file mode I'll need custom differential tests that run both binaries and compare the actual file contents. The pattern should be: use DiffTest when the reference binary is available (skipping if not found), then add separate direct functional tests that verify behavior without needing a reference binary at all.\n\nSetting up the test file structure...\n\nI need to resolve the reference sponge binary and skip tests if it's not available, then implement the main sponge functionality to read stdin, handle atomic writes to output files, support append mode, and manage signal cleanup.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the buffer strategy—Go's bytes.Buffer handles dynamic growth automatically, which satisfies the requirement even if it doesn't explicitly start at 8192 bytes. For spilling to a temp file when memory gets too large, I'll set a reasonable threshold around 256MB rather than trying to query runtime stats or RLIMIT_DATA.\n\n